---
title: "Reactive cells: a dependency graph"
---

`{js}` cells form a dependency graph through `//| viewof` (a named input control),
`//| name` (a value a cell publishes), and `//| input` (what a cell consumes). Moving a
slider re-runs only the cells downstream of it, in dependency order, including cells that
depend on it *transitively* through a derived value.

```{js}
//| viewof: n
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range"; input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.step = "1"; input.value = "3";
const wrap = document.createElement("label");
wrap.style.cssText = "display:flex;gap:.6rem;align-items:center";
wrap.append("n", input);
return wrap;
```

The next cell publishes a derived value, `squared`, that consumes the slider `n`:

```{js}
//| name: squared
//| input: n
return tali.value("n") ** 2;
```

This cell consumes `squared` — a derived `//| name`, not the slider itself — so it stays
in sync only because the change propagates transitively (`n → squared → here`):

```{js}
//| input: squared
const p = document.createElement("p");
p.textContent = "n squared = " + tali.get("squared");
return p;
```

A second, independent slider drives its own cell, untouched when `n` moves:

```{js}
//| viewof: m
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range"; input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.step = "1"; input.value = "5";
const wrap = document.createElement("label");
wrap.style.cssText = "display:flex;gap:.6rem;align-items:center";
wrap.append("m", input);
return wrap;
```

```{js}
//| input: m
const p = document.createElement("p");
p.textContent = "m doubled = " + (tali.value("m") * 2);
return p;
```
